## Teardown and restoration

  * `no radius-server local` was the case's own STEP 7, so nothing further had to be undone.
  * `rcdiff.py work/pre-u2.log work/post-u2.log` -> **no output, exit 0: the post-case
    running-config is byte-identical to the pre-case running-config**, including the L3
    underlay (vlan10 10.38.22.34/27, vlan48 10.38.22.66/27), which was never touched.
  * `show boot` is unchanged: boot image flash:/x960-tb277.rel, boot config flash:/default.cfg
    (re-read in the post capture; gate 7 satisfied).
  * Nothing was written to startup-config. No device was reloaded or power-cycled.
  * Nothing was started on the box that is still running: every capture was a bounded
    `timeout <secs> tcpdump` and every send was a one-shot process. No RADIUS server of ours
    ran during this case (the DUT was the server).
  * /dev/u3 (swi_b) was never opened by this case; /dev/u5 and /dev/u1 were never opened at all.

## NOT MEASURED, and why (stated so no reader infers it was covered)

  * **Persistence across a reboot.** `satisfactory-level-of-testing` asks for it, and it was
    deliberately NOT done. The DUT's L3 underlay (vlan10/vlan48) exists in running-config only
    and its boot config is `flash:/default.cfg`, so a reload would destroy the path that the
    rest of this campaign's groups depend on, and `copy running-config startup-config` would
    make a bench-level change to the Test Engineer's standing configuration that is beyond
    this case (STANDING-ORDERS §4). Reported to the sentinel as a NEEDS TEST ENGINEER note.
  * **EAP-MD5, EAP-TLS, EAP-PEAP and 802.1X supplicant authentication.** The server reports
    them enabled; proving them needs an 802.1X supplicant on a switched port, which is a
    different test set (STANDING-ORDERS §6). PAP and the MAC-format user were proven instead.
  * **`nas <ip> message-authenticator auto|optional|required`.** The DUT logged a BlastRADIUS
    warning for every request this case sent without a Message-Authenticator, and the NAS
    defaulted to `auto`. Sending one (radsend `--msg-auth`) and watching the warning stop is a
    worthwhile follow-up; it was not run here.
  * **`group`, `vlan` and `domain-style`** attributes of the local server.

